ID: B2-Ch06a-Intro
TEXT:

The girl in the silk pajamas watches us with a smug, triumphant grin, plainly thrilled with herself for hauling us into the air. Despite having the whole group suspended several feet above the cobblestones, her aura gives off no trace of casting. Even when Kate and Flower dampen their presence, a careful read still catches the faint hum of a mage. The girl in front of us feels like any ordinary human off the street. Either her aura suppression leaves Flower and Kate in the dust, or...

"Hey, I know you!" Arraka's voice screeches from the amulet. "You're that stillwater girl who kept trailing behind Eiden and the fox during the Still Winter! What was the name... Melenda? Milandra?..."

"Melindra," the girl says, voice dropping flat and cold. She snaps a finger, and a sharp gust clicks the golden lid of Arraka's amulet shut.

Muffled cursing rattles inside the metal casing, but nobody pays it any mind. With the banshee boxed, Melindra's bright smile returns instantly.

"So, as I was saying," Melindra continues, "there is something I want to ask of you. I came to watch you in the arena because word got around that you were convicted for killing a Varathian noble. I thought--"

"Hold on a second," Hadrik cuts in. "You're a stillwater?"

"Well, yes," Melindra says, blinking. "I thought that part was fairly obvious. Arraka literally just said--"

"How did you become one?" Hadrik asks. "Were you born like that? Can women be born as stillwaters?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch06a-Intro2, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Intro2, v_checkpoint_rich = 0)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Intro2
TEXT:

"No, of course not!" Melindra snaps, her irritation flaring. "Nobody is born a stillwater. I became one the exact same way everyone else did."

"Everyone?" I ask. "Even Eiden? Is Eiden an artificial mage too? Wasn't he born with magic?"

"What in the world is an artificial mage?" Melindra throws her hands up. "Eiden was born a mage. He became a stillwater later, like the rest of us. Why is this so hard to grasp?"

"How?" I press. "How does someone become a stillwater?"

"Listen to me," Melindra says. "If you think I'm standing here answering your questions before you even hear what I'm--"

"How do you know Eiden?" Daren cuts in. "Are you working with him?"

"No, I am not working with Eiden!" Melindra groans, stomping empty air. "If I had Eiden helping me, why would I come begging you? Can all of you just shut up for ten seconds and let me finish one damn senten--"

"If you want a favor, set us down first," Kate says, cold and even. "Dangling people in midair is a poor way to ask for help."

Melindra glares at Kate with a pout that rivals Flower on a bad afternoon. After a stubborn pause, she waves one hand. The cushion of air beneath our boots thins, dropping us in a smooth glide until our soles meet the ground.

She could probably snap our necks with a flick of her wrist, but standing there in custom sleepwear, scowling like a scolded teenager, she comes across as the least intimidating caster I have ever laid eyes on. Less threatening than Flower. Less threatening than the sweating ambassador from Ollendor.

"There," Melindra huffs as the wind dies completely. "You are back on the ground. Now, am I allowed to speak, or are you going to interrupt me with more questions?"

choice(""Actually, I have a few more questions!"", B2-Ch06a-Questions, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Questions)
choice(""Go on, we're all listening."", B2-Ch06a-Listening, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Listening)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Questions
TEXT:

"Actually, I have a few more questions!"

"What?" Melindra strains to keep her voice level. "What questions now?"

"For starters," I say, "you never answered how someone becomes a stillwater."

"I don't understand," Melindra says, squinting at us. "Why ask me? Shouldn't you be asking Eiden? Aren't you his followers?"

"Hell no!" Daren roars.

Melindra blinks, genuine bewilderment washing over her face.

"His friends, then?" she asks.

"Far from it," Kate says.

"Then how did you break that noble's shield?" Melindra asks. "Eiden gave you the power to do it, didn't he?"

"He did," Kate says.

"Why give you that kind of power if you aren't his friends?"

"You've known Eiden for centuries," Kate says, flat. "Does he strike you as someone who requires a logical reason for anything he does?"

"Well..." Melindra trails off, looking down. "Not lately, no. But if you truly aren't his friends, I'm amazed he left you breathing."

"Aren't we all?" Kate says.

"What is it you want from us, Melindra?" Daren asks, folding his arms.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch06a-Assassins, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Assassins)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Listening
TEXT:

"Go on, we're all listening."

"Good!" Melindra's sunny expression bounces back. "As I was saying, if I could reach Eiden myself, I wouldn't be standing here bothering you. But he's been completely ignoring me since I returned to Varathia, so I thought you, as his followers, might--"

"What did you just call us?" Daren asks, his brow furrowing deep.

"Eiden's followers," Melindra says. "Aren't you?"

"Hell no!" Daren roars.

Melindra blinks, genuine bewilderment washing over her face.

"His friends, then?" she asks.

"Far from it," Kate says.

"Then how did you break that noble's shield?" Melindra asks. "Eiden gave you the power to do it, didn't he?"

"He did," Kate says.

"Why give you that kind of power if you aren't his friends?"

"You've known Eiden for centuries," Kate says, flat. "Does he strike you as someone who requires a logical reason for anything he does?"

"Well..." Melindra trails off, looking down. "Not lately, no. But if you truly aren't his friends, I'm amazed he left you breathing."

"Aren't we all?" Kate says.

"What is it you want from us, Melindra?" Daren asks, folding his arms.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch06a-Assassins, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Assassins)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Assassins
TEXT:

"Oh, right!" Melindra's grin returns in full. "In short, I want you to kill someone for me."

"Whoa, whoa, hold on," Daren says, raising a hand. "I am stopping you right there. Regardless of what you heard from the stands, none of us are assassins for hire. We don't take lives without serious justification, and we certainly aren't murdering someone just because you asked."

"I'm not asking for charity!" Melindra says. "I am more than willing to compensate you."

"We don't need gold," Daren says. "We entered a mage tournament, remember?"

"I know that!" Melindra says. "My payment is tournament progress. Don't you want to know where to find the glowing pinecones?"

"Why would we need you for that?" Daren asks. "They're pinecones. How difficult can it be to pick one up?"

Melindra smirks. She digs into a pocket and pulls out a single small seed. The moment it rests against her palm, a brilliant yellow radiance flares from the shell, pulsing with magic--the exact hue Golmyck described when announcing the first trials. She extends her open hand toward Daren.

"Go on," she says with a smile. "Take it. You'll understand why you need my help soon enough."

Daren takes the seed from her fingers. The bright yellow glow dies instantly. He rolls the seed between his palms, channeling what he can, but the husk stays dark and dead.

"As you can see," Melindra says, thoroughly pleased with herself, "the seeds don't light up for just anyone. They only resonate with auras packed with absurd amounts of raw power. Among tournament contestants, I doubt anyone besides the four sages has enough magical capacity to make one glow on touch. Without that, finding a glowing pinecone is pure blind luck. Pass it around if you don't believe me. Not one of you will get a flicker."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch06a-Venture, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Venture)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Venture
TEXT:

We pass the seed from hand to hand. Kate holds it, Flower takes it, Hadrik squeezes it, and I cup it in my palm. Nothing happens. The shell remains completely dark. None of us carry anywhere near the magical power required to awaken the resonance.

"So," Daren says, handing the dark seed back to Melindra, "am I to understand you're offering us one of these pinecones in exchange for the assassination?"

"Oh, no," Melindra says, pocketing the seed. "I don't have a whole pinecone. I found a few loose seeds on the forest floor near the border of a sacred wood on my way here. I wouldn't dare push deeper into the trees to track down an actual cone. The golden fox despises me."

"Why a sacred forest?" I ask. "Can't they be found anywhere else?"

"They only grow in the sacred groves," Melindra says. "Every last one belongs to the golden fox."

"Oh, come on!" Hadrik groans loudly. "We barely crawled out of those woods a few days ago, and now we have to march right back in? What is wrong with these organizers? Do they want anyone to survive this tournament?"

"If you don't have a pinecone," Kate says, "and you have no intention of guiding us through the fox's territory, what exactly are you offering?"

"Information," Melindra says, beaming. "There is a device engineered specifically to resonate with the pinecones. It triggers the glow without needing a massive aura, as long as you get close enough. It's incredibly rare, but I know who holds one, and I can lead you right to them once my business is settled. I cannot share their name, but I am confident I can talk them into lending it to you for a few days. That should give you more than enough time to secure a pinecone."

choice(""Why can't you tell us who this person is? Is it someone we know?"", B2-Ch06a-Ollendor, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Ollendor, v_b2_ch6_person = 1)
choice(""Why would they lend us such a rare device for free?"", B2-Ch06a-Ollendor, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Ollendor, v_b2_ch6_person = 0)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Ollendor
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch6_person == 1) {
"Why can't you tell us who this person is? Is it someone we know?"

"I very much doubt any of you have crossed paths with them," Melindra says. "The reason I can't share their identity is that they specifically asked me to keep it quiet whenever I speak to outsiders. Don't read too much into it. They're just the secretive sort. That's all."

"Right..." I say.

"So, do we have a deal?" Melindra asks.

"I'm sorry, Melindra," Daren says, "but I cannot go around killing people to advance my own goals. Even if my ultimate purpose is to aid the entire world, I will not murder a man simply because you want him dead."

"So you need a moral justification?" Melindra asks, tilting her head with interest. "What if I tell you the man I want dead is a tyrant who has slaughtered thousands of innocents, ruling his kingdom through terror and bloodshed? Would that change your mind?"

"Who are you talking about?" Kate asks.

"Why, the brother of the man you killed a few days ago!" Melindra says. "The king of Ollendor."

}
#if(v_b2_ch6_person == 0) {
"Why would they lend us such a rare device for free?" I say. "They don't even know who we are!"

"They know who I am," Melindra says. "And if they refuse, we can simply steal it. I'm a stillwater. What are they going to do to stop me?"

"Right..." I say.

"So, do we have a deal?" Melindra asks.

"I'm sorry, Melindra," Daren says, "but I cannot go around killing people to advance my own goals. Even if my ultimate purpose is to aid the entire world, I will not murder a man simply because you want him dead."

"So you need a moral justification?" Melindra asks, tilting her head with interest. "What if I tell you the man I want dead is a tyrant who has slaughtered thousands of innocents, ruling his kingdom through terror and bloodshed? Would that change your mind?"

"Who are you talking about?" Kate asks.

"Why, the brother of the man you killed a few days ago!" Melindra says. "The king of Ollendor."

}
"You want us to assassinate a king of Varathia?" Hadrik asks, eyes wide. "Have you lost your mind? We were thrown into the arena for killing a noble, and now you want us to butcher a monarch?"

"Don't worry about that," Melindra says breezily. "Ollendor had nothing to do with organizing this tournament. Nobody wants anything to do with them. Even Golmyck is more respected by the other rulers. None of the organizers will care if you cause chaos inside his borders. The only reason they threatened you with disqualification over the earl was that you killed him inside Thilias. Start a riot in Ollendor, and nobody on this continent will bat an eye. Except the local garrison, of course."

"I'm not sure how I feel about that," I say.

"She's telling the truth," Leila writes, glowing blue Common forming above her palms. "Ollendor is a rogue kingdom now. Neither the lessathi nor the other kings have any sway over what happens inside its borders. Everyone ignores them, and most kingdoms have already expelled their ambassadors. That's why my father fled there after escaping Thilias. It was the one place nobody would search for him."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch06a-Motives, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Motives)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Motives
TEXT:

"See?" Melindra gestures at Leila's floating words. "Nothing to worry about!"

"Ollendor is a long march from here," Daren says. "And then you have to guide us to this friend with the detector. That's a massive detour when we're already three days behind every other contestant."

"So what?" Melindra says. "Nobody has made any real progress. None of the contestants know pinecones are locked to the sacred groves. Most are avoiding the fox's territory entirely because they're terrified of her. The odds of anyone finding a pinecone this week are practically zero. Even when they figure out where to look, they'll have to face the golden fox directly. Without a detector, sneaking through to test every cone by hand is impossible. They will have to fight her and her beasts, and you know how that ends."

"I still don't understand," Kate says, narrowing her eyes. "You're a stillwater. Why do you need our help to kill a king?"

"Because I entered Varathia as an official tournament participant!" Melindra throws up her hands. "It was the only way I could get back onto the island after that miserable king exiled me. But the tournament's geas prevents me from harming any locals. I've been stuck here since the first day, hunting for someone who can do the job in my place."

"And what guarantee do we have that you'll honor your word once the king is dead?" Daren asks. "For that matter, what keeps you from pulling an Eiden and slaughtering us for amusement halfway down the road? We know you two have a history."

"For crying out loud!" Melindra groans in exasperation. "If you don't trust me, bind me with an oath. You're an enchanter, aren't you? Make me swear to uphold my end of the bargain and promise not to hurt you. Then we can finally get moving."

"An enchanter's oath?" Daren asks, taken aback. "Are you serious? You understand you will die if you break it, right?"

"Of course I know that!" Melindra snaps.

"And do you expect us to swear a reciprocal oath not to harm you?" Daren asks.

"No, you don't need to bother," Melindra says. "It's not as if any of you could hurt me anyway."

"Right..." Daren mutters.

He turns to face the rest of us.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch06a-Payback, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Payback)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Payback
TEXT:

"What do you think?" Daren asks, turning to us. "Putting aside her infuriating condescension, the offer is surprisingly solid. At the very least, she isn't trying to trick us, and we get to free a kingdom from a tyrant along the way."

"Well," Hadrik rumbles, scratching his beard, "I've never toppled a throne before. It'll make a hell of a tavern tale over ale. Certainly beats wandering around kicking pinecones."

"Taking everything into account," Kate says, "her plan gives us an edge over the other contestants. The organizers clearly rigged this round so only the four sages can qualify."

"What is the point of that, anyway?" Hadrik scowls. "I'm not gunning for the trophy, but it's insulting. Why invite hundreds of mages if you never planned to give them a fair shot? Is it all just theater?"

"You forget," Illuna's voice cuts in from the shared body, "the objective requires delivering the pinecone to Galamir. That means the sages can be ambushed on the road and their pinecones stolen. Anyone strong or clever enough to rob a sage can still advance, even without the power to ignite the seed themselves. Contestants can also pledge themselves to a sage's retinue. There are multiple paths forward, but all of them collide with the sages. If you want to avoid that slaughter, the detector is your best alternative."

"I don't know about the rest of you," I say, "but I'm looking forward to causing some chaos in the ambassador's homeland. A little payback for the arena feels long overdue. Besides, Ollendor is going to be Leila's new home. We might as well clean up the neighborhood before she settles in."

Leila blinks, startled. Her eyes widen as the realization hits her: she doesn't have to part ways with us now that the arena games are finished.

"You are coming with us to Ollendor, right?" I ask her.

"Yes!" Leila writes rapidly, a bright smile lighting up her face. "Yes, of course!"

"If everyone agrees," Daren says, drawing his broadsword, "we proceed with the enchanter's oath. Does anyone have terms to add? Melindra has agreed not to harm us and to guide us to the device once the king is dead, but we should make sure the wording leaves no loopholes."

choice(""The deal should not be considered over until the device is actually in our hands."", B2-Ch06a-Doubting, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Doubting, v_b2_ch6_hurt = 0)
choice(""Melindra should swear that her friend with the device won't try to hurt us, either."", B2-Ch06a-Doubting, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Doubting, v_b2_ch6_hurt = 1)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Doubting
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch6_hurt == 0) {
"The deal should not be considered over until the device is actually in our hands."

"Naturally," Daren says. "If she expects us to take down a royal court, I'm not doing it on the vague promise of directions."

"Fair enough," Melindra says, shrugging.

Daren checks that no one else has additions, then uses the tip of his blade to make a shallow nick on Melindra's left forefinger. She smears a drop of crimson across the steel. A clean, brilliant white light flares along the edge.

"Is the link active?" Melindra asks. "May I speak?"

"The enchantment is set," Daren says. "Begin."

"Right," Melindra says. "First, I swear I will not bring harm to any of you."

"You know how this binding works, don't you?" Daren warns. "If you set no duration, the oath is permanent. If I seal this, you can never harm any of us for the rest of your life."

"Listen," Melindra points at him. "If we stand here second-guessing every breath, we will never leave Thilias. Seal the vow."

"If you insist," Daren mutters, hovering his palm over the glowing blade. The white radiance pulses.

"Wait, stop!" Melindra yanks her hand back.

"Are you serious?" Daren grunts, barely breaking the flow in time. "What now?"

"Can I change 'harm' to 'inflict lethal injury'?" Melindra asks. "I don't want to drop dead for clapping someone on the shoulder."

"It is an enchanter's binding!" Daren snaps. "Harm means genuine harm. A pat on the back or a light shove won't trigger it. You receive a severe aura warning long before the magic bites. You cannot trigger it by accident."

"Oh," Melindra says. "Fine. Proceed."

Daren seals the first vow, the light steadying before he nods for the second.

"Right," Melindra says. "I swear that once you slay the king of Ollendor, I will lead you directly to the owner of the pinecone detector without unnecessary delay, taking no rest unless you request it. I also swear that once we reach them, I will not rest until the device is safely in your hands."

}
#if(v_b2_ch6_hurt == 1) {
"Melindra should swear that her friend with the device won't try to hurt us, either."

"Good catch," Daren says. "Her personal vow is useless if she leads us straight into an ambush."

"I cannot fully control my acquaintance," Melindra says, "but I will swear to defend you from them if they turn hostile, and our contract remains open until you are in a safe location with the detector in your grasp."

"That works," Daren says.

Daren checks that no one else has additions, then uses the tip of his blade to make a shallow nick on Melindra's left forefinger. She smears a drop of crimson across the steel. A clean, brilliant white light flares along the edge.

"Is the link active?" Melindra asks. "May I speak?"

"The enchantment is set," Daren says. "Begin."

"Right," Melindra says. "First, I swear I will not bring harm to any of you."

"You know how this binding works, don't you?" Daren warns. "If you set no duration, the oath is permanent. If I seal this, you can never harm any of us for the rest of your life."

"Listen," Melindra points at him. "If we stand here second-guessing every breath, we will never leave Thilias. Seal the vow."

"If you insist," Daren mutters, hovering his palm over the glowing blade. The white radiance pulses.

"Wait, stop!" Melindra yanks her hand back.

"Are you serious?" Daren grunts, barely breaking the flow in time. "What now?"

"Can I change 'harm' to 'inflict lethal injury'?" Melindra asks. "I don't want to drop dead for clapping someone on the shoulder."

"It is an enchanter's binding!" Daren snaps. "Harm means genuine harm. A pat on the back or a light shove won't trigger it. You receive a severe aura warning long before the magic bites. You cannot trigger it by accident."

"Oh," Melindra says. "Fine. Proceed."

Daren seals the first vow, the light steadying before he nods for the second.

"Right," Melindra says. "I swear that once you slay the king of Ollendor, I will lead you directly to the owner of the pinecone detector without unnecessary delay, taking no rest unless you request it."

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch06a-Pajama, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Pajama)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Pajama
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch6_hurt == 0) {
Daren sweeps his hand over the steel, locking the second vow into the enchantment. The white light recedes into the metal until the blade returns to plain polished steel.

"Is that everything?" Melindra asks. "Are we finished?"

"We are," Daren says, sheathing his sword. "The oath is bound."

"Excellent!" Melindra's sunny smile returns. "So our company consists of you, the ice mage, the girl with the banshee, the giant in dwarf shape, and--"

Her finger halts mid-air as she points toward me. Her voice cuts off. She stands frozen, staring straight at my chest.

"The dwarf is a giant?..." Leila writes in glowing letters, but none of us are looking at her text.

Melindra marches straight up to me, brow furrowed in fierce concentration. She halts less than a foot away, scanning me from boots to collar.

"Hmm..." she murmurs, tapping her chin as she looks me over.

At this distance, the details stand out. She has a pretty face framed by light-brown hair pulled into a high ponytail. While wearing silk pajamas in the middle of a city square looks absurd on paper, her clothes are tailored with far more deliberate care than Eiden's shapeless rags. The fabric is cut close, accentuating her slender frame and curves while leaving her completely free to move.

"Hmm..." Melindra tilts her head, leaning in uncomfortably close, her eyes narrowing as she inspects my aura.

}
#if(v_b2_ch6_hurt == 1) {
"Not quite," Daren says. "You must also swear that once we reach this person, you will ensure they lend us the detector, and the contract is not complete until the device rests in our hands."

"Fine," Melindra huffs. "I swear it."

Daren sweeps his hand across the crossguard, sealing the second condition.

"For your third and final vow," Daren says, "do you swear to protect us from this contact or any associates if they attack, and that our agreement is only fulfilled once you leave us in a safe haven with the detector in our possession?"

"Yes, I swear," Melindra says.

Daren passes his palm over the blade a final time. The white light recedes into the steel until the weapon returns to normal.

"Is that everything?" Melindra asks. "Are we finished?"

"We are," Daren says, sheathing his sword. "The oath is bound."

"Excellent!" Melindra's sunny smile returns. "So our company consists of you, the ice mage, the girl with the banshee, the giant in dwarf shape, and--"

Her finger halts mid-air as she points toward me. Her voice cuts off. She stands frozen, staring straight at my chest.

"The dwarf is a giant?..." Leila writes in glowing letters, but none of us are looking at her text.

Melindra marches straight up to me, brow furrowed in fierce concentration. She halts less than a foot away, scanning me from boots to collar.

"Hmm..." she murmurs, tapping her chin as she looks me over.

At this distance, the details stand out. She has a pretty face framed by light-brown hair pulled into a high ponytail. While wearing silk pajamas in the middle of a city square looks absurd on paper, her clothes are tailored with far more deliberate care than Eiden's shapeless rags. The fabric is cut close, accentuating her slender frame and curves while leaving her completely free to move.

"Hmm..." Melindra tilts her head, leaning in uncomfortably close, her eyes narrowing as she inspects my aura.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch06a-Mixture, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Mixture)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Mixture
TEXT:

"Is there something on my--" I start, but she cuts me off.

"Turn around!"

Before I can move, she grabs my shoulders and bodily spins me around. She pauses, studying the flow of my aura from behind, then lets out a sharp gasp.

"What?" I ask, turning back to face her. "What is it?"

"You're a half-lessathi!" Melindra's eyes light up with genuine excitement. "That's why your aura felt so familiar! I'm half-lessathi too! It has been ages since I met anyone of our blood. There were thousands of us before the Still Winter, but the lessathi clamped down on their breeding rules after the war, and their own numbers dwindled. You almost never run into half-lessathi anymore."

She pauses, her expression shifting.

"Wait," she murmurs. "So that's why Eiden took an interest in you."

"What do you mean?" I ask. "What does my heritage have to do with anything? Is it related to the stat booster?"

"No," Melindra says. "Nothing to do with your device."

"Then what is it?"

"I'm sure you'll piece it together eventually," Melindra says with a teasing smirk.

"You stillwaters never give a straight answer, do you?"

She grins and lets the question hang.

"Still, I'm surprised you managed to purchase a stat booster," Melindra says. "I wanted to see how a device would interact with my half-lessathi aura during the tournament, so I tried buying one before traveling here. Every shop was completely empty."

"On which continent?" I ask. "I bought mine on the Western Continent. Where I grew up, you could find them in almost every pawnshop and curiosity stall."

"I searched the Western Continent as well," Melindra says. "Across multiple cities. Every merchant told me their stock ran dry weeks before I arrived."

"Wait," I say, frowning as the memory surfaces. "I had a harder time finding mine than expected, too. I had to comb through several districts, and that was over a month ago. The merchants grew tense the moment I asked about restocks, like they were afraid of someone finding out. Do you think someone pressured them into pulling the devices off the market?"

"Never mind that now," Melindra says, waving a dismissive hand. "Let me see your stat booster. I want to test how it reacts to my aura."

choice(""Yeah, but make sure that you don't mess with my stats!"", B2-Ch06a-Distinction, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Distinction, v_b2_ch6_stat = 1)
choice(""Actually, I'd rather keep my stat device, if you don't mind."", B2-Ch06a-Distinction, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Distinction, v_b2_ch6_stat = 0)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Distinction
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b2_ch6_secret,1) if (v_b2_ch6_stat == 1)
achievement("Secret code discovered",v_ac_b2_ch6_secret)
#if(v_b2_ch6_stat == 0) {
"Actually, I'd rather keep my stat device, if you don't mind."

"Fine!" Melindra crosses her arms with a pout. "I didn't need your toy anyway."

Leila steps forward, lightly tugging Melindra's sleeve.

"Yes?" Melindra looks down at her.

"I have a question," Leila writes, the blue letters hanging between them. "Can you distinguish between a full lessathi and a half-lessathi solely by the feel of their aura?"

"Of course," Melindra says. "I've encountered plenty of half-lessathi in my lifetime. You need a sharp magical sense to catch the shift, but the difference is distinct."

}
#if(v_available_points == 22 && v_b2_ch6_stat == 1) {
"Yeah, but make sure that you don't mess with my stats!"

"Naturally," Melindra says. "One quick test and you get it right back."

I pull the device from my pocket and hand it over.

The screen lights up. Melindra stares at the numbers, her eyebrows creeping toward her hairline. She looks up at me in disbelief.

"Are you serious?" she asks. "You haven't invested a single point?"

"I like living on the edge," I tell her.

"Right..." She watches me for a moment, then turns her attention back to the bronze casing.

Her thumbs dance over the side toggles, clicking the sequence with practiced speed.

"Hold on," I warn. "You promised not to--"

"Hush," Melindra says, her expression deadly serious.

After a tense silence, she sighs and shoves the device back into my hands, scowling.

"Nothing," she mutters. "My aura must have shifted too drastically when I became a stillwater. The system no longer recognizes me as half-lessathi."

"What were you doing?" I ask. "I saw your fingers flying, but the values didn't budge."

"It's a diagnostic override," Melindra says. "A sequence that temporarily spikes hearing by several points to test whether the user's physical senses respond to the device. It only functions on hearing, and only once a day for a few seconds."

"What is the input sequence?" I ask.

"Three left, three right, two left, four right, then left-right-left-right," she says. "You must complete the whole pattern within five seconds or the override resets."

"Three left, three right, two left, four right, left-right-left-right," I repeat. "Got it. Thanks."

"Don't mention it," Melindra says.

Leila steps forward, lightly tugging Melindra's sleeve.

"Yes?" Melindra looks down at her.

"I have a question," Leila writes, the blue letters hanging between them. "Can you distinguish between a full lessathi and a half-lessathi solely by the feel of their aura?"

"Of course," Melindra says. "I've encountered plenty of half-lessathi in my lifetime. You need a sharp magical sense to catch the shift, but the difference is distinct."

}
#if(v_b2_ch6_stat == 1 && v_available_points != 22) {
"Yeah, but make sure that you don't mess with my stats!"

"Naturally," Melindra says. "One quick test and you get it right back."

I pull the device from my pocket and hand it over. Her thumbs dance over the side toggles, clicking the sequence with practiced speed.

"Hold on," I warn. "You promised not to--"

