}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch06a-Shopping, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Shopping)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Shopping
TEXT:

"Could you..." Leila's glowing text pauses, the letters shimmering in the air before she finishes. "Could you tell me if I am half-lessathi as well?"

"You?" Melindra tilts her head.

She steps close to Leila, brow furrowing as she focuses her senses on the younger girl's presence.

"Hmm..." Melindra hums softly after a moment. "No. You are full lessathi through and through. The signature is unmistakable."

"I see..." Leila writes, a quiet, relieved smile softening her features. "Thank you."

Watching her shoulders drop, I remember what she told me about the Beacon outcasts--how the other lessathi branded her a broken half-blood simply because she was born mute. The sting of their cruelty had cut far deeper than she ever let on.

"Well," Melindra says, turning back to the group. "That settles our business for today. If there are no objections, I want us on the road at the crack of dawn."

"We still need exit papers from the king before we can clear the gates," Daren says. "We have an audience later today, but there's no guarantee the clerks will finish the paperwork before evening. Wait a moment."

Daren slips off his pack, digs out an enchanted metal transceiver, and holds it out to her.

"Take this. We will contact you if the schedule shifts. The enchantment spans long distances, so it will reach you anywhere within city limits."

"Fair enough," Melindra says, pocketing the device. "If the plan holds, meet me at the northern gate at sunrise. Ollendor lies north, so we leave from the closest exit."

"Agreed," Daren says.

"See you tomorrow," Melindra says with a casual wave, then heads down the street.

"Damn it," I mutter as she turns the corner. "She still didn't tell us how someone becomes a stillwater."

"You have weeks on the road to interrogate her," Daren says. "We're traveling together, remember?"

"Right," I say.

"We should head toward the palace," Daren adds. "The king has an army to manage, and I doubt he'll wait around for us."

"He told me to wait at least an hour before heading up while he reorganized his garrison," I say. "Let's check the markets in the meantime. Once we leave Thilias, we won't see another proper merchant stall for a long time."

"Good point," Daren agrees.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch06a-Absorbed, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Absorbed)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Absorbed
TEXT:

#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 0) {
"Since you are staying the night in Thilias," Enrique beams, "there is no longer any excuse to skip our celebration! I know the finest culinary masters in the city, and my cellar is packed with aged vintages. Tonight we feast like emperors!"

Daren sighs, rubbing his temples.

"Do as you wish," he says. "Just don't get carried away. We march before dawn."

We wave goodbye to Enrique and set off toward the palace at a steady walk. The moment we clear the square, muffled shrieks rattle from Flower's golden amulet.

"What is it, Arraka?..." Flower sighs, unlatching the lid.

"The absolute nerve of that brat!" Arraka's voice roars out, three distinct voices grating against each other in overlapping fury. "Did you see that? Snapping my lid shut without so much as a greeting! Is that any way to treat someone you've known for six centuries?"

}
#if(v_ch11_saved_rose == 1) {
"I'll meet you back at the estate!" Rose says with a warm smile. "If this is your final night in Thilias, I'll make sure a proper feast is waiting when you return from the palace!"

"We'll be looking forward to it!" Hadrik grins.

We wave goodbye to Rose and set off toward the palace at a steady walk. The moment we clear the square, muffled shrieks rattle from Flower's golden amulet.

"What is it, Arraka?..." Flower sighs, unlatching the lid.

"The absolute nerve of that brat!" Arraka's voice roars out, three distinct voices grating against each other in overlapping fury. "Did you see that? Snapping my lid shut without so much as a greeting! Is that any way to treat someone you've known for six centuries?"

}
"I've been meaning to ask," I say, leaning toward the amulet. "What is with your voice? Most of the time you sound like one person, but every so often three separate voices talk at once. Is that an old-age banshee quirk?"

"Age has nothing to do with it," Arraka snaps. "Those extra voices belong to the first two spirits I devoured while still on the magical plane. Elkira and Rinaru, I think. It has been thousands of years. The names blur together."

"The first two?" I ask. "You absorbed more than two spirits while you were over there?"

"Hundreds of them," Arraka says casually. "The extraction technique was still experimental when I applied it to Elkira and Rinaru, which is why their impressions still leak through. I perfected the process soon after, so the rest went down smoothly."

"Didn't the other spirits try to stop you?"

"Of course they did," Arraka laughs. "Every soul made me stronger. By the time the heavy hitters realized what was happening, I was already far beyond anything they could handle."

choice(""What happens to the spirits that you absorb?"", B2-Ch06a-Amass, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Amass, v_b2_ch6_banned = 0)
choice(""Is this the reason why you got banned from the magical plane?"", B2-Ch06a-Amass, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Amass, v_b2_ch6_banned = 1)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Amass
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch6_banned == 0) {
"What happens to the spirits that you absorb? Are they still alive inside of you?"

"In a sense," Arraka says. "Their ethereal matter is fused into mine, completely dormant and devoid of thought. The process is irreversible. They might as well be dead."

"What did the gods do about it?" I ask. "They reside on the magical plane too, don't they? Why didn't they join forces to wipe you out?"

"You think they didn't try?" Arraka snorts. "The 'gods' are nothing special. Just magical spirits like the rest of us. They only adopted those grandiose titles after discovering they could siphon energy from mortals on the earthen plane through worship. Once the prayers started rolling in, the arrogance went straight to their heads. All that divine power didn't save them when I slaughtered half their kind single-handed."

"You slaughtered half the gods on the magical plane?"

"The lesser ones, mostly," Arraka admits. "The ones with small cults. The heavier ones--Death, Fate, Time--held their ground. We hit a stalemate: I hunted more spirits to absorb, and they scrambled for more mortal worship."

"Was that when they exiled you to the earthen plane?"

"Later," Arraka says. "When they realized their mortal flock couldn't keep pace with my absorption rate, they convened a grand council of every major power in the realm, including entities who refused the god title, like the golden fox. That council decreed my banishment, along with every female spirit belonging to what you mortals call 'civilized races'--humans, elves, gnomes, and the rest."

}
#if(v_b2_ch6_banned == 1) {
"Is this the reason why you got banned from the magical plane?"

"Part of it," Arraka says. "Mostly it was the slaughter."

"The slaughter?..."

"There isn't much to do on the magical plane," Arraka says. "Most spirits exist solely to maintain their own existence. Dying of old age is impossible, so unless something kills you, you live forever. That's what made butchering them so hilarious. Mortals expect death eventually, but spirits fancy themselves eternal. The absolute panic in their final moments is unlike anything you'll ever witness down here. Smashing that delusion is endlessly satisfying."

"What did the gods do about it?" I ask. "They reside on the magical plane too, don't they? Why didn't they join forces to wipe you out?"

"You think they didn't try?" Arraka snorts. "The 'gods' are nothing special. Just magical spirits like the rest of us. They only adopted those grandiose titles after discovering they could siphon energy from mortals on the earthen plane through worship. Once the prayers started rolling in, the arrogance went straight to their heads. All that divine power didn't save them when I slaughtered half their kind single-handed."

"You slaughtered half the gods on the magical plane?"

"The lesser ones, mostly," Arraka admits. "The ones with small cults. The heavier ones--Death, Fate, Time--held their ground. We hit a stalemate: I hunted more spirits to absorb, and they scrambled for more mortal worship."

"Was that when they exiled you to the earthen plane?"

"Later," Arraka says. "When they realized their mortal flock couldn't keep pace with my absorption rate, they convened a grand council of every major power in the realm, including entities who refused the god title, like the golden fox. That council decreed my banishment, along with every female spirit belonging to what you mortals call 'civilized races'--humans, elves, gnomes, and the rest."

}
choice(""Wait, you have different races in the magical plane?"", B2-Ch06a-Elf, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Elf, v_b2_ch6_fox = 0)
choice(""The fox originated from the magical plane? How did she end up here, then?"", B2-Ch06a-Elf, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Elf, v_b2_ch6_fox = 1)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Elf
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch6_fox == 0) {
"Wait, you have different races in the magical plane?"

"The exact same races you have down here," Arraka says. "Monsters and beasts included. We don't have physical bodies over there, but you can read species from an aura just as easily as on the earthen plane."

"Hold on," I say. "Does that mean our spirits travel to the magical plane when we die?"

"No," Arraka says. "Mortals don't have spirits. You only have souls. When you die, the ethereal substance of your soul dissolves and disperses back into the ethereal plane. The same happens to our spirits when destroyed. It's possible recycled soul matter eventually binds to newborn spirits in the magical plane, but that is pure theory. None of us carry past-life memories from your world."

"Getting back to your exile..." I say. "Why did the council banish every female spirit instead of just you?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch6_fox == 1) {
"The fox originated from the magical plane? How did she end up here, then?"

"Why don't you ask her yourself?" Arraka says. "Aren't you two friends now?"

"'Friends' is a massive stretch," I tell her.

"All I know," Arraka says, "is that long before my banishment, the fox developed a bizarre fascination with the beasts of Varathia. For reasons nobody understood, the fauna here showed far higher intelligence than creatures on other continents. The fox constantly complained about how poorly mortals treated them. I don't know the exact year she crossed over, but she bound herself to the local flora and fauna in a symbiotic pact--her power protects them, and their life force anchors her physical body. Beyond that, you'll have to ask her. I was sealed under a mountain for most of it."

"Getting back to your exile..." I say. "Why did the council banish every female spirit instead of just you?"

}
"Because I was only devouring female spirits from the civilized races," Arraka says. "The council panicked, assuming the rest of them might turn out like me if left unchecked. In truth, I only targeted those spirits because their aura frequencies matched mine closely enough to absorb, being an elf myself. There was nothing inherently dangerous about them. The risk of someone replicating my method still exists, but the rest of the plane is far too thick-headed to reverse-engineer a spell that intricate."

"Wait," I say. "You're an elf?"

"Of course I am an elf!" Arraka snaps. "What did you think I was?"

"I assumed banshees were their own species," I say. "I never gave it much thought."

"Banshees are not a species!" Arraka says. "It is a crude earthen label slapped on us after we were dumped here. Aura readings make our lineage obvious. How abysmal is your magical sense? Illuna is an elf as well, and I doubt you caught that either."

"Petal is an elf?" Flower asks, wide-eyed.

"Why the shock?" Illuna's dry voice cuts in from the shared body. "You have a clear projection of my form inside your own head. Don't tell me you failed to notice my pointed ears for twenty years."

"I saw them," Flower says, "but I thought I just imagined you that way! I didn't know it was real!"

"So all this time we treated you like dangerous monsters," I say, "you were just elven spirits?"

"Precisely," Illuna says.

"It never ceases to amaze me how blind mortals are to auras," Arraka laughs. "No wonder you haven't solved why your women cannot be born as casters. If you can't distinguish an elf from a ghoul, you have zero hope of unraveling the root."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch06a-Funnel, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Funnel)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Funnel
TEXT:

"Did you..." I pause, sorting the pieces in my head. "Are you implying that the reason women cannot be born as mages on the earthen plane is directly tied to your exile?"

Kate, who had been walking in silence, snaps her head around. Her gaze fixes on the amulet, sharp and intense.

"Took you long enough, Barry-boy," Arraka sneers. "What finally tipped you off? The fact that every banshee is a humanoid female spirit? Or that female beasts and monsters cast magic naturally, while human women cannot cast a spark without drastic aura alterations? Or perhaps the glaring fact that women only gain magic when merged with a banshee? Flower is right in front of you. Her affinity is fire; Illuna's is water. Flower didn't inherit water magic from Petal. To draw energy from the magical plane, a mortal must forge a resonant link with a spirit of similar nature. You didn't think human mages siphon raw magic without an ethereal spirit serving as a funnel, did you?"

"So every mage on our plane is linked to an otherworldly spirit?" I ask.

"Subconsciously," Arraka says. "Since the connection spans planes, it requires no intimacy. Most casters form the link instinctively at birth without either party ever realizing it. But you cannot forge a subconscious bond if every compatible female spirit was ripped from the magical plane and dumped down here. You can still force magic through artificial modifications, of course, but that warps your aura completely. Like that stillwater girl. Or your ice-wielding friend."

"How did they banish an entire population of spirits across planes?" I ask. "It can't be as simple as opening a rift and shoving you through."

"Memphir's design, from what I gather," Arraka says. "The God of Fate always loved overly convoluted schemes, even if half of them blew up in his face."

"The God of Fate?"

"The one obsessed with illusions," Arraka says. "I don't know the exact ritual, but they tampered with the underlying mechanism that distributes newborn spirits across planes, reprogramming it so female spirits of the civilized races manifest on the earthen plane instead."

"You can alter reality's systems like that?"

"It requires catastrophic amounts of energy," Arraka says, "but the cowards rallied enough backing to force the change."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch06a-Nest, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Nest)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Nest
TEXT:

"Why didn't you reverse it after arriving?" I ask. "You absorbed hundreds of spirits--shouldn't you have the power?"

"I cannot reach the mechanism from here!" Arraka snaps. "To undo Memphir's tamper, I would need direct access to the Magium, which is impossible from the earthen plane."

"The Magium?..."

"Well, not the whole Magium," Arraka says. "You understand what I mean."

"No. I really don't."

"The node within the Magium responsible for generating spirits and feeding them into the magical plane," Arraka explains. "Spirits don't reproduce like mortals, despite being male and female. There is a core mechanism that produces new spirits to replace the fallen, keeping the realm's population stable. Memphir tampered with that core. Once altered, it flushed existing female spirits down here along with every generation created since. And from Varathia, the only Magium conduits I can tap are entirely useless for reversing the shift."

"Conduits? How large is the Magium?" I ask, thoroughly baffled. "Isn't it a singular artifact hidden on Varathia?"

Arraka falls dead silent. We all pause, waiting.

"Did..." she sputters, her voice wavering. "Did you just ask me how big the Magium is?"

"Yes," I say.

A heartbeat later, Arraka bursts into hysterical laughter. The howling shriek echoes off the stone walls, roaring for ten full seconds without drawing breath.

"Illuna! Did you hear him?" Arraka gasps between shrieks. "He asked how big the Magium is! Ahahahaha!"

"Stop laughing and answer me!" I snap. "Tell me what you know!"

"You crossed oceans to fight for the Magium and you don't even know what it is?" Arraka howls. "If I had lungs I'd be weeping! You fools actually sailed here without a clue! Ahahahaha!"

"Shut up!" I yell. "Are you going to explain it or not?"

She ignores me completely, lost in a fit of wild mockery.

choice("Threaten to spin her amulet until she gives me the answer.", B2-Ch06a-Threaten, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Threaten)
choice("Wait for her to stop laughing and then ask her again.", B2-Ch06a-Wait, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Wait)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Threaten
TEXT:

"Do you want me to spin your amulet until you give me my answer?"

"Are you sure about that, Barry-boy?" Arraka's laughter cuts off sharply. "Confident you'll never need my senses again? You enjoy being ambushed by invisible monsters that much?"

"Leave it, Barry," Daren says, placing a heavy hand on my shoulder. "If she refuses to speak, no amount of dizziness will change her mind. We will uncover the Magium on our own."

"I know what the Magium is!" Flower pipes up.

"You do?" I turn to her.

"Yes," Flower says.

"What is it?"

"I can't tell you," Flower says simply.

"Why not?"

"I'm not allowed to."

"Not allowed by whom?" I press. "The kings? Are the rulers of Varathia forbidding you from telling contestants?"

"I... cannot answer that," Flower says, shifting uncomfortably.

"What happens if you tell us?"

"We die," Illuna's cold voice breaks through. "Instantly. We drop dead before the explanation leaves our mouth."

"Why would you drop dead from answering a question?" Hadrik asks, scratching his head.

"An enchanter's oath," Daren says grimly. "Someone bound them to silence under penalty of death. They risk their lives even hinting at it. Stop pressing them before Flower slips and triggers the curse."

"Damn it," Hadrik grunts. "I thought we finally had a lead."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch06a-Talkative, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Talkative)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Wait
TEXT:

I wait out the fit in silence until her shrieks taper off into dry snickers.

"Now that you're finished," I say, "are you going to tell us what the Magium is?"

"No," Arraka says.

"Why not?"

"Where is the fun in handing you the answers?" Arraka sneers. "You have to earn it. Isn't that why you came?"

"Leave it, Barry," Daren says, placing a hand on my shoulder. "If she refuses to speak, nothing we say will change her mind. We will uncover the Magium on our own."

"I know what the Magium is!" Flower pipes up.

"You do?" I turn to her.

"Yes," Flower says.

"What is it?"

"I can't tell you," Flower says simply.

"Why not?"

"I'm not allowed to."

"Not allowed by whom?" I press. "The kings? Are the rulers of Varathia forbidding you from telling contestants?"

"I... cannot answer that," Flower says, shifting uncomfortably.

"What happens if you tell us?"

"We die," Illuna's cold voice breaks through. "Instantly. We drop dead before the explanation leaves our mouth."

"Why would you drop dead from answering a question?" Hadrik asks, scratching his head.

"An enchanter's oath," Daren says grimly. "Someone bound them to silence under penalty of death. They risk their lives even hinting at it. Stop pressing them before Flower slips and triggers the curse."

"Damn it," Hadrik grunts. "I thought we finally had a lead."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch06a-Talkative, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Talkative)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Talkative
TEXT:

"Why the long face?" Daren asks. "You were never chasing the Magium. You told us yourself you entered for the fights."

"True enough," Hadrik grins, "but imagine the bragging rights if we snatched the Magium before the tournament even wrapped up!"

"Not going to happen," Daren says firmly. "Even if we knew what it was, it is undoubtedly fortified or hidden beyond reach. We focus on our assigned trials, not on circumventing the rules. Let's move. We have provisions to buy before meeting the king."

As we head toward the palace district, Kate and Leila walk shoulder to shoulder, but neither says a word. Daren drops back, matching pace with Hadrik and me, and lowers his voice.

"Barry," Daren murmurs, "weren't Kate and the girl supposed to be childhood companions?"

"In a sense," I say. "They grew up at the Beacon together, but Leila was kept isolated from the rest because she was born lessathi."

"Still," Daren says, glancing ahead, "after all these years, you'd think they would have something to say. Maybe we should intervene. Help break the ice."

"Leave them be," I tell him. "Neither of them is the chatty type, and Leila has to write every word in the air. Give them space to find their footing."

"You know," Hadrik chimes in, "if Leila is lessathi, shouldn't you pass along Eiden's message? The one about the Still Winter?"

"Good call," I say. "I'll tell her right now."

"Barry, wait--" Daren starts, but I am already calling out.

"Hey, Leila!"

Leila turns, glowing blue letters drifting before her. "Yes?..."

"I have a message for you from Eiden," I say.

"Eiden?" Leila writes, tilting her head. "The stillwater who challenged the dragon in the arena?"

"That's him," I say. "He says to remember the Still Winter."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch06a-Message, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Message)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Message
TEXT:

"Uh... all right?..." Leila writes, looking utterly baffled.

"Ignore him, Leila," Kate cuts in with an exasperated glare. "Barry is just being an idiot again."

Leila turns back to her, and Kate begins explaining who Eiden is and why the phrase carries weight.

"Damn it, Barry!" Daren hisses under his breath. "Can you take that message seriously for once? We were nearly killed the last time Eiden brought it up."

"Relax," I whisper back. "That contract expired. I was supposed to deliver his words to the first lessathi I encountered. Those were Diane's associates in the forest. Eiden almost crushed us because I broke the deadline. The fact that he let it slide proves he never actually cared about the delivery. If he truly wanted the message spread, he could have teleported straight to them and spoken it himself--he recognized their presence from miles away. He's probably forgotten the whole thing by now."

"I hope you're right," Daren mutters.

"Besides," I say, nudging his shoulder, "my timing was deliberate. Look."

Ahead of us, Kate and Leila are deep in conversation. The topic has clearly shifted from ancient wars to what an unbearable fool I am.

#if(v_b2_ch5_scimitar == 2) {
"See?" I whisper. "Common ground."

Half an hour later, we reach the grand central bazaar on the road to the castle. We split into smaller groups, agreeing to rendezvous at the central fountain once our business is concluded. Kate accompanies Leila to hunt for spare clothes and a proper traveler's pack. Daren and I head toward the smithies so I can upgrade my crossbow and Daren can purchase enchanted whetstones. Flower and Illuna wander off toward the apothecaries for potions, while Hadrik heads straight for the market fountain to lounge in the sun.

Before entering the armory, I draw the two captured scimitars and hand them to Daren for appraisal.

"Hmm," Daren hums, tracing the fuller with a fingertip. "Both carry standard enchantments for edge retention and structural durability. But the blades are forged from genuine elven steel, and the balance is exceptional. Don't let a merchant take either for less than fifty western gold coins."

Inside the weapons stall, I open negotiations with the blacksmith. After several minutes of back-and-forth haggling, I walk away having sold one scimitar for seventy gold pieces while keeping the second at my hip. With coin in my pocket, I move over to the ranged racks to inspect the crossbows.

}
#if(v_b2_ch5_scimitar == 1) {
"See?" I whisper. "Common ground."

Half an hour later, we reach the grand central bazaar on the road to the castle. We split into smaller groups, agreeing to rendezvous at the central fountain once our business is concluded. Kate accompanies Leila to hunt for spare clothes and a proper traveler's pack. Daren and I head toward the smithies so I can upgrade my crossbow and Daren can purchase enchanted whetstones. Flower and Illuna wander off toward the apothecaries for potions, while Hadrik heads straight for the market fountain to lounge in the sun.

Before entering the armory, I draw the captured scimitar and hand it to Daren for appraisal.

"Hmm," Daren hums, tracing the steel. "Sharpness and durability weaves. Forged from high-grade elven steel with superb balance. Are you planning to sell it?"

"I'm keeping it," I say. "I wanted to know how it stacks up against the dagger you enchanted for me. They seem roughly on par."

"They are," Daren says. "The metal quality matches. The extra reach will serve you well."

Inside the shop, I ask the armorer where he keeps his repeating mechanisms and head straight to the ranged racks.

}
#if(v_b2_ch5_scimitar == 0) {
"See?" I whisper. "Common ground."

Half an hour later, we reach the grand central bazaar on the road to the castle. We split into smaller groups, agreeing to rendezvous at the central fountain once our business is concluded. Kate accompanies Leila to hunt for spare clothes and a proper traveler's pack. Daren and I head toward the smithies so I can upgrade my crossbow and Daren can purchase enchanted whetstones. Flower and Illuna wander off toward the apothecaries for potions, while Hadrik heads straight for the market fountain to lounge in the sun.

Inside the armory, I ask the smith where he keeps his repeating mechanisms and head straight to the ranged racks.

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch06a-Crossbow, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Crossbow)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Crossbow
TEXT:

#if(v_ch10_gems == 1 || v_ch3_treasure != 0) {
I am looking for a repeating crossbow with a magazine capacity of at least five bolts, matching the cadence of my old weapon.

Two models on the display rack stand out immediately, both far superior to my worn piece. One commands a significantly higher price, but with the pouch of gems and ancient coin resting in my pack, the difference in cost is negligible.

The premium model holds seven bolts in its internal magazine, boasting reinforced steel limbs for greater draw weight, extended range, and tighter accuracy. The cheaper model fires six bolts and improves on my old gear in every respect, but lacks the polished mechanism of the seven-shot piece.

I scan the rest of the ranged inventory, but the other models are either overpriced junk or worse than what I carry. The choice comes down to the two repeating crossbows on the rack.

}
