ID: B2-Ch06a-Elixir
TEXT:

achievement("Living on the edge",v_ac_b2_ch6_edge)
#if(v_b2_ch6_crossbow == 0) {
I settle on the six-shot model. It outperforms my old weapon by a wide margin, and I am not entering a marksman competition. Saving coin makes more sense.

Once the transactions are settled and the new weapon is stowed in my pack, Daren and I step back into the street. I remember an essential supply I still need, tell Daren to head ahead to the fountain, and duck into the apothecary shop across the lane.

"Barry?" Flower looks up from a row of glass phials. "Didn't you go for weapons?"

"I did," I say. "Remembered one last errand."

"What are you buying?"

"...An anti-venom elixir," I mutter, rubbing the back of my neck.

Flower's eyes flash vivid blue. Illuna takes the front, staring at me in deadpan silence.

}
#if(v_b2_ch6_crossbow == 1) {
I purchase the seven-shot repeating crossbow. Since it serves as my sole ranged option in a fight, cutting corners on quality is a mistake.

Once the transactions are settled and the new weapon is stowed in my pack, Daren and I step back into the street. I remember an essential supply I still need, tell Daren to head ahead to the fountain, and duck into the apothecary shop across the lane.

"Barry?" Flower looks up from a row of glass phials. "Didn't you go for weapons?"

"I did," I say. "Remembered one last errand."

"What are you buying?"

"...An anti-venom elixir," I mutter, rubbing the back of my neck.

Flower's eyes flash vivid blue. Illuna takes the front, staring at me in deadpan silence.

}
"Pick up an anti-disease draught while you have your coin out," Illuna says dryly. "They cost more than anti-venom and only counter basic ailments, but dying of a common jungle fever after surviving the arena would be humiliating."

I pay the apothecary ten copper pieces for the anti-venom vial and a silver coin for the anti-disease tonic, downing both on the spot. Illuna does the same with her purchases. We exit the shop and make our way to the central fountain, where Daren and Hadrik are locked in a loud argument over who would win a duel between Azarius, Sage of the West, and Talmak, Sage of the North.

"Listen to me," Hadrik insists as we walk up. "Azarius knows a mountain of spells, fine. But in terms of raw, unbridled power, Talmak crushes him. The man is an earth elementalist! What good is a fancy incantation when two massive slabs of granite grind you to paste?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch06a-Complex, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Complex)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Complex
TEXT:

"Hadrik," Daren sighs, "I understand your loyalty to Talmak because you share dwarven blood, but that is simply not how high-tier spellcraft operates. Raw power alone does not guarantee victory in a duel. Versatility decides the outcome. Azarius is a master generalist; his repertoire spans nearly every school, which makes his offense impossible to predict. Specialists are always at a disadvantage against generalists. That is precisely why I trained in both enchantment and white magic. Confining yourself to a single discipline narrows your tactical options against veteran casters."

"Come off it!" Hadrik waves a thick hand. "Talmak isn't limited to tossing boulders. You saw him at the sage exhibition last year. He hurled lightning bolts and threw up force barriers with the best of them!"

"Basic weaves," Daren counters. "Fundamental spells. He has never demonstrated an advanced incantation outside the earth element."

"Why bother with complicated weaves?" Hadrik grins. "When you have a bottomless well of energy, you pump power into a simple blast until it hits like a siege engine!"

"That is an atrocious waste of magical energy!" Daren groans. "Advanced spells were developed precisely for their efficiency."

"Why would Talmak care about efficiency?" Hadrik laughs. "He has energy to burn! When has anyone ever seen him tire in a fight? For all we know, his aura recovers faster than he can spend it!"

"Barry," Daren turns to me, throwing his hands up. "Can you believe this stubborn dwarf? He honestly thinks Talmak could take Azarius in a serious duel."

choice(""He does have a point, though. I mean, Azarius is pretty old."", B2-Ch06a-Old, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Old)
choice(""Yeah, I honestly couldn't imagine Azarius ever losing in a fair fight."", B2-Ch06a-Fair, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Fair)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Old
TEXT:

"He does have a point, though. I mean, Azarius is pretty old."

"Hah!" Hadrik barks in triumph. "See? Even Barry gives it to Talmak, and he hails from the Western Continent!"

"Being from the West doesn't obligate him to back Azarius," Daren mutters. "I stand by my assessment. Talmak would make it a brutal fight, but if Azarius commits, the Sage of the West takes the match."

"Fine, fine," Hadrik grunts. "How about another match? Who takes the crown between Eiden and Arraka?"

"Didn't they already settle that centuries ago?" I ask. "During the Still Winter. Eiden tore her apart."

"From what the legends say," Hadrik rubs his beard, "it was Eiden and the golden fox against Arraka. Two on one."

"Arraka was fused with a stillwater host at the time," I point out. "Two against two. That is as fair a duel as you get in war. Eiden completely dismantled her."

"Nobody dismantled me!" Arraka's screech erupts from the amulet. "Therius was dead weight! You cannot compare a second-rate host to the golden fox, let alone Eiden!"

"If he was such dead weight," I say, "why did you fail to fully claim his body during your awakening?"

"That had nothing to do with power, you ignorant ape!" Arraka roars. "Didn't Illuna explain ethereal mechanics to you? The host holds home-ground advantage inside his own mind! In a straight clash of auras, I would have ground him to dust! And Eiden with him!"

"You claim that," I say, "yet you ended up sealed in an amulet."

"Because Eiden relies on filthy tricks!" Arraka snarls. "In pure, unadulterated magical capacity, I dwarf him!"

"Raw power is useless if your application is sloppy," I counter.

"This is not about technique, you moron!" Arraka shrieks. "He cheats! He exploits flaws in the fabric of magic itself!"

"What does that even mean?"

"Ask him yourself!" Arraka snaps. "He has been standing right beside you with his presence masked for the past twenty seconds. I'm sure he has plenty of leisure to explain."

The air beside me shimmers. Eiden drops his invisibility weave, floating an inch above the flagstones with his hands resting in his pockets. His eyes remain shut, wearing his usual calm, polite smile.

"You never let me enjoy the show, do you, Arraka?" Eiden murmurs.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch06a-Calamity, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Calamity)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Fair
TEXT:

"Yeah, I honestly couldn't imagine Azarius ever losing in a fair fight."

"Bah!" Hadrik grumbles. "Asking Barry is rigged. He was born on the Western Continent. Of course he backs his home sage."

"Nonsense," Daren says. "I was raised on the Southern Continent, and if you pitted Azarius against the Sage of the South, I would still put my gold on Azarius without hesitation."

"Fine, fine," Hadrik grunts. "How about another match? Who takes the crown between Eiden and Arraka?"

"Didn't they already settle that centuries ago?" I ask. "During the Still Winter. Eiden tore her apart."

"From what the legends say," Hadrik rubs his beard, "it was Eiden and the golden fox against Arraka. Two on one."

"Arraka was fused with a stillwater host at the time," I point out. "Two against two. That is as fair a duel as you get in war. Eiden completely dismantled her."

"Nobody dismantled me!" Arraka's screech erupts from the amulet. "Therius was dead weight! You cannot compare a second-rate host to the golden fox, let alone Eiden!"

"If he was such dead weight," I say, "why did you fail to fully claim his body during your awakening?"

"That had nothing to do with power, you ignorant ape!" Arraka roars. "Didn't Illuna explain ethereal mechanics to you? The host holds home-ground advantage inside his own mind! In a straight clash of auras, I would have ground him to dust! And Eiden with him!"

"You claim that," I say, "yet you ended up sealed in an amulet."

"Because Eiden relies on filthy tricks!" Arraka snarls. "In pure, unadulterated magical capacity, I dwarf him!"

"Raw power is useless if your application is sloppy," I counter.

"This is not about technique, you moron!" Arraka shrieks. "He cheats! He exploits flaws in the fabric of magic itself!"

"What does that even mean?"

"Ask him yourself!" Arraka snaps. "He has been standing right beside you with his presence masked for the past twenty seconds. I'm sure he has plenty of leisure to explain."

The air beside me shimmers. Eiden drops his invisibility weave, floating an inch above the flagstones with his hands resting in his pockets. His eyes remain shut, wearing his usual calm, polite smile.

"You never let me enjoy the show, do you, Arraka?" Eiden murmurs.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch06a-Calamity, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Calamity)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Calamity
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch1_interfere == 0) {
"Again?" Daren's voice drops, his hand dropping toward his hilt. "Good gods, will you ever stop stalking us?"

"There is no need for hostility," Eiden says softly. "I traveled here purely to offer congratulations on your performance in the coliseum."

"Right," Hadrik grunts, eyes narrowing. "And that's the sole reason you dropped by?"

"Since you press the matter, there is one other detail," Eiden says. "I observed your party heading toward the royal palace. Golmyck hasn't tasked you with some grand civic duty, has he?"

"We are going to collect travel papers," Daren says flatly.

"Is that so?" Eiden tilts his head. "If the king offers you any commission concerning the safety or future of Thilias's citizens, do not waste your time accepting it."

"Why not?" Daren demands.

"Because Thilias will not stand for much longer," Eiden says pleasantly. "Nor will any other city on this island."

"What..." Daren's voice wavers, the color draining from his face. "What are you talking about?..."

}
#if(v_b2_ch1_interfere == 1) {
"Again?" Daren's voice drops, his hand dropping toward his hilt. "Good gods, will you ever stop stalking us?"

"There is no need for hostility," Eiden says softly. "I traveled here purely to offer congratulations on your performance in the coliseum."

"Right," Hadrik grunts, eyes narrowing. "And that's the sole reason you dropped by?"

"Since you press the matter, there is one other detail," Eiden says. "I observed your party heading toward the royal palace. Golmyck hasn't tasked you with some grand civic duty, has he?"

"We are going to collect travel papers," Daren says, keeping his expression neutral.

"Indeed?" Eiden's smile sharpens with amused disbelief. "In either case, if the king offers you any commission concerning the safety or future of Thilias's citizens, do not waste your time accepting it."

"Why not?" Daren demands.

"Because Thilias will not stand for much longer," Eiden says pleasantly. "Nor will any other city on this island."

"What..." Daren's voice wavers, the color draining from his face. "What are you talking about?..."

}
"Shortly before this tournament reaches its conclusion," Eiden says, "a catastrophe will sweep the land, eradicating every single city across the continent simultaneously."

"And you plan to stand by and watch millions die?" Daren asks, horror lacing his voice. "Aren't you supposed to be the Creator of this land?"

"A common historical error," Eiden says mildly. "The island of Varathia predates my existence by centuries. Mortals bestowed the title upon me to mark a new era, because the golden fox and I laid the foundations of the civilization that rose from the Still Winter. And you misunderstand my role. I have no intention of standing idly by while the cities burn. Their destruction will occur as a direct consequence of my own actions."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch06a-Tantrum, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Tantrum)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Tantrum
TEXT:

"You monster!" Daren's voice shakes with fury, his knuckles white on his grip. "I will not let you do this!"

"Daren," Eiden says, his tone soothing and patronizing, "I expected you to rejoice. Weren't you the champion preaching that salvation is always possible if you refuse to give up? I have delivered your salvation. A clean end, freeing them from their own misery. A swift death is far more merciful than what most of these wretched creatures deserve, and as for the rest--"

"Shut up," I cut in.

Eiden's chin lifts. "Pardon?"

"I'm sick of your whining," I tell him. "You act like a spoiled child throwing a tantrum. The moment the game stops going your way, you smash the board, break every piece, and expect the world to praise your brilliance. You pulled the exact same stunt at Olmnar. Is leveling a city your answer to everything you fail to control?"

Dead silence grips the square. Daren, Kate, Hadrik, and Flower stare at me in stunned disbelief. Eiden turns his face fully toward me. His eyes remain shut, but the polite smile vanishes completely.

"Explain yourself," Eiden says, the calm in his voice razor-sharp.

"You spent centuries building this civilization," I say, "and the moment it slips from your grasp, you decide you don't care anymore and resolve to butcher everyone down to the last child. You failed. You held godlike power in your hands and still couldn't build what you wanted. Instead of owning your failure and fixing the cracks, you sweep the ruins into a mass grave to pretend none of it was your fault. You aren't being merciful. You're throwing a fit because you were inadequate to the task."

"I have heard enough of your posturing," Eiden says coldly. "You lecture without understanding what festers in these cities. You believe slavery and blood arenas are the depths of Varathian rot? You have no conception of what transpires behind the walls of the other kingdoms. The depravity to which these people sink would turn your stomach."

"So what?" I say. "Aren't you the one who founded this place? Isn't a ruler supposed to guide his people? How did you let it rot? Did you abandon them to their own devices without lifting a finger to govern? Even I could have managed this continent better, and I know nothing about statecraft."

"You claim you could have steered them with my power?" Eiden's voice drops to a lethal whisper. "You, who failed to deliver a simple sentence to a lessathi?"

"I'm saying I could do a better job with no magic at all!" I snap back. "You had every advantage in existence and all you accomplished was compounding the disaster. What was Olmnar about, anyway? Did you think burning a city to ash would win back the golden fox's approval? Is that what you told yourself? Stop running from your own wreckage and stop being a coward!"

Eiden's brow furrows.

The world locks solid.

Sound vanishes. Dust motes freeze in mid-air. Daren, Kate, Hadrik, Flower, and Leila stand rigid as stone statues. Eiden steps forward, drifting across the frozen flagstones toward me, the air around him crackling with suppressed fury.

choice("Try to break the time freezing spell by stepping out of its range", B2-Ch06a-Range, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Range)
choice("Stand my ground and do not avert my eyes from Eiden", B2-Ch06a-Avert, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Avert)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Range
TEXT:

I recognize this weave. It matches the temporal spell the booster gave me during the fight against Tyrath. If I can move beyond the focal boundary, the spell collapses instantly, locking the caster out of the weave for the rest of the day.

I push off my back foot to leap backward, but my shoulder slams hard against an invisible barrier.

Eiden stands less than a foot in front of me. His eyelids are open, his dark pupils boring into mine with glacial fury.

"What is the matter, Barry?" Eiden's voice is quiet and terrible. "Was I not the coward? Why are you retreating?"

"I'm not retreating," I say, meeting his gaze. "I'm looking for the boundary to break your spell."

"It has been a very long time since anyone provoked me to anger," Eiden murmurs. "I must credit you, Barry. You possess an extraordinary talent for pushing past all restraint. Do you court death at every turn? Is nonexistence truly what you desire?"

"You aren't going to kill me," I say.

"Why shouldn't I?"

"Because you know I'm right," I say, keeping my voice steady. "Every word struck home. If I were wrong, you wouldn't be standing here with your eyes open, furious enough to freeze the square. Killing me now just proves my point, and your pride won't allow that."

#if(v_ch11_difference != 1) {
"You truly enjoy gambling with your life," Eiden says, studying my face. "What infuriates me most is that you actually believe what you preach. You genuinely believe you could accomplish the impossible--that you could reshape mortal nature without a scrap of my power. On what foundation do you build such arrogance?"

"On the fact that I have spent my entire life chasing the impossible," I say. "And unlike you, I have never walked away. Look at me! Who else is stubborn enough to throw themselves into a lethal tournament across the ocean just to chase a childhood dream? Yet here I am."

I hold his gaze, refusing to blink.

"I don't care about the philosophy of mortal nature," I continue. "I saw people here treated like livestock and slaughtered in arenas for entertainment. That is all I need to know that this island must change. If you have given up on fixing it, I will do it myself. Power or no power, I am going to succeed where you failed, and then I will shove every single victory in your face."

}
#if(v_ch11_difference == 1) {
"You truly enjoy gambling with your life," Eiden says, studying my face. "What infuriates me most is that you actually believe what you preach. You genuinely believe you could accomplish the impossible--that you could reshape mortal nature without a scrap of my power. On what foundation do you build such arrogance?"

"On the fact that I have spent my entire life chasing the impossible," I say. "And unlike you, I have never walked away. Look at me! Who else is stubborn enough to throw themselves into a lethal tournament across the ocean just to chase a childhood dream? Yet here I am."

I hold his gaze, refusing to blink.

"I don't care about the philosophy of mortal nature," I continue. "I saw people here treated like livestock and slaughtered in arenas for entertainment. That is all I need to know that this island must change. If you have given up on fixing it, I will do it myself. Power or no power, I will do it. I told you once before: I am going to accomplish everything you couldn't, and then I will shove every single victory in your face."

}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch06a-Loopholes, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Loopholes)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Avert
TEXT:

I plant my boots and stare straight into his eyes as he glides toward me. He stops a foot away. His eyelids part, revealing dark, piercing irises brimming with quiet menace.

"It has been a very long time since anyone provoked me to anger," Eiden murmurs. "I must credit you, Barry. You possess an extraordinary talent for pushing past all restraint. Do you court death at every turn? Is nonexistence truly what you desire?"

"You aren't going to kill me," I say.

"Why shouldn't I?"

"Because you know I'm right," I say, keeping my voice steady. "Every word struck home. If I were wrong, you wouldn't be standing here with your eyes open, furious enough to freeze the square. Killing me now just proves my point, and your pride won't allow that."

