}
choice("Continue", B2-Ch06a-Loopholes, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Loopholes)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Loopholes
TEXT:

As I finish speaking, the murderous glare fades from Eiden's eyes, replaced by cool appraisal. As we stand face to face, I track his right arm--every few moments, he subtly extends it out to the side, then pulls it back.

"One of the great advantages of refined aura perception," Eiden says calmly, "is that you can detect not only deceit, but the slightest hesitation from the tremors in a person's aura. The fact that your aura showed neither lie nor hesitation throughout that speech is genuinely fascinating."

He pauses, extending his right arm sideways once more before turning.

"Here is our arrangement," he says, closing his eyes as he resumes a slow, measured pacing within a narrow radius. "The tournament will force your party to visit several cities. You are bound for Galamir, and with Melindra at your side, Ollendor is inevitable. Other kingdoms will follow as the trials advance. I will monitor your actions from afar to see what change you can effect in the time allotted. If you prove true to your words and enact meaningful reform through your own strength, I will grant you the power required to avert the calamity. Afterward, I will leave the fate of Varathia entirely in your hands. Should that miracle occur, I will--"

"By the gods!" I shout, cutting him off. "Arraka was telling the truth! You really are cheating!"

Eiden halts mid-stride. "I beg your pardon?"

"I know why you keep reaching your right arm out!" I say. "And why you've been pacing the exact same three-foot circle! It's the boundary warning! I used this exact time-freezing spell back when you powered up my stat device. The spell restricts the caster to a tight radius. If any limb crosses the perimeter, a ten-second cancellation warning triggers."

"And how does triggering a safety warning benefit me?" Eiden asks, tilting his head.

"Because the weave's architect never accounted for the warning timer when calculating total duration!" I tell him. "As long as you cross the threshold and step back on the ninth second, you append ten extra seconds to the spell each time. The time limit was put there as a safeguard for the caster, not because the magic demands it. You can stretch the freeze indefinitely as long as you keep the rhythm! That is what Arraka and Tyrath meant by tricks. You aren't transcending the laws of magic through divine power. You're taking advantage of technicalities its designers never bothered to close. You're using loopholes!"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch06a-Formidable, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Formidable)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Formidable
TEXT:

"While I applaud your deductive reasoning," Eiden says, his voice flat, "I fail to see how exposing a minor technicality aids you in--"

"You did the same thing to Daren, didn't you?" I interrupt. "Putting him to sleep through layered wards wasn't raw omnipotence. You exploited another flaw!"

"Indeed?" Eiden's brows lift slightly, his eyes still closed. "And what flaw was that?"

I turn the mechanics over in my mind, linking his aura reads with Daren's armor.

"Protection overload," I say. Eiden's expression tightens minutely. "That's it. An enchanter who stacks too many overlapping defensive matrices without balancing the frequencies creates resonant friction. The wards interfere with each other, tearing minute gaps in the defensive perimeter. Anyone who can read aura currents can slip a sleep weave straight through the tear. Daren over-stacks wards constantly."

"Extraordinary," Eiden says, his polite smile returning. "I have employed that vulnerability against formidable archmages for centuries, and not one unravelled the mechanism until today. Yet I must still ask: how does this knowledge protect you? Even if you master every loophole I possess, my raw magical capacity still dwarfs nearly every entity on this continent. You are in no better position now than you were on our second day."

"Yes, I am," I say. "Because now I know you aren't invincible. If you relied on loopholes to win your battles, your raw power wasn't enough. You have vulnerabilities. And anything with a weakness can be killed."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch06a-Accomplish, v_current_scene = B2-Ch06a-Accomplish)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Accomplish
TEXT:

Eiden chuckles, a soft sound that carries no warmth.

"So you intend not only to redeem what I could not salvage, but to strike me down in open combat. And here I assumed the remainder of this tournament would prove entirely tedious."

He pauses, the smile settling.

"Very well. I shall watch your progress with great interest. My gaze will follow you through the cities. Are there any further questions before I depart?"

"Just one," I say. "What is the Magium?"

Eiden's grin widens.

"You didn't honestly expect me to hand you that answer, did you?"

"No," I say. "Worth asking, though."

Eiden offers a final incline of his head and vanishes without a whisper.

The temporal lock shatters. Sound and movement crash back into the square all at once. Daren, Hadrik, and Kate whip their heads around, weapon hilts gripped tight, searching for the stillwater who was standing among us a heartbeat ago.

"He's gone..." Daren mutters, blinking at the empty air. He turns, his gaze locking on me. "And Barry is the only one who looks completely unbothered. Is there something you want to share with the group?"

"Daren," I say, stepping forward. "I know you have a hundred questions, and I will answer every one of them. But first, do something for me."

"What?" Daren asks, frowning.

"Take out your parchment and ink," I tell him. "Write down every defensive enchantment you had active on our second day on this island. We have work to do."

choice("Next chapter", B2-Ch07a-Intro, v_next_chapter_crash = 1, v_current_scene = B2-Ch07a-Intro, v_chapter_save_counter = 5, v_checkpoint_rich = 1, special:checkpoint_save)


ID: B2-Ch06a-Credits
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch1_interfere == 1 && v_second_book_purchased == 0) {
The seventh chapter is now available.

